public int minTime(int n, int[][] edges, boolean[] hasApple) {
    // Initialize a graph with n nodes and edges
    Graph graph = new Graph(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < edges.length; i++) {
        graph.addEdge(edges[i][0], edges[i][1]);
    }

    // Initialize a boolean array to keep track of which nodes have apples
    boolean[] hasApple = new boolean[n];
    for (int i = 0; i < hasApple.length; i++) {
        hasApple[i] = false;
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < edges.length; i++) {
        if (hasApple[edges[i][0]] || hasApple[edges[i][1]]) {
            hasApple[edges[i][0]] = true;
            hasApple[edges[i][1]] = true;
        }
    }

    // Perform a BFS search from node 0 to collect all the apples
    int[] dist = new int[n];
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        dist[i] = -1;
    }
    dist[0] = 0;
    Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();
    queue.add(0);
    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
        int node = queue.poll();
        for (int neighbor : graph.getNeighbors(node)) {
            if (dist[neighbor] == -1) {
                dist[neighbor] = dist[node] + 1;
                queue.add(neighbor);
            }
        }
    }

    // Calculate the minimum time required to collect all the apples and return to node 0
    int minTime = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (hasApple[i]) {
            minTime += dist[i];
        }
    }
    return minTime;
}
```
This function first initializes a graph with the given edges and a boolean array to keep track of which nodes have apples. It then performs a BFS search from node 0 to collect all the apples and calculates the minimum time required to collect all the apples and return to node 0.

The time complexity of this function is O(n+m), where n is the number of nodes and m is the number of edges. The space complexity is O(n), where n is the number of nodes.

