public String getSmallestString(int n, int k) {
    if (n < 1 || k < 1) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("n and k must be positive");
    }
    if (n == 1) {
        return String.valueOf((char) ('a' + k - 1));
    }
    int quotient = k / 26;
    int remainder = k % 26;
    if (remainder == 0) {
        quotient--;
        remainder = 26;
    }
    return String.valueOf((char) ('a' + quotient - 1)) + getSmallestString(n - 1, remainder);
}
```
Explanation:
The function first checks that n and k are positive. If n is 1, the function returns the character at position k in the alphabet.

If n is greater than 1, the function calculates the quotient and remainder of k divided by 26. If the remainder is 0, the function decrements the quotient and sets the remainder to 26.

The function then returns a string consisting of the character at position quotient in the alphabet, followed by the result of calling itself recursively with n - 1 and the remainder.

